  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Sasanquasaponin I and Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP through a multi-step pathway. Sasanquasaponin I is directly linked to Microtubule-associated protein 1B, which is then targeted by two distinct compounds: (2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[(2R,3R,4R,6R)-6-[[(3S,8R,9S,10R,13R,14S,17S)-14-hydroxy-17-[(1S)-1-hydroxyethyl]-10,13-dimethyl-1,2,3,4,7,8,9,11,12,15,16,17-dodecahydrocyclopenta[a]phenanthren-3-yl]oxy]-4-methoxy-2-methyloxan-3-yl]oxy-4-methoxy-6-methyloxane-3,5-diol and (2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[[(2S,3S,4aS,6aS,8R,10aR,10bS)-3-ethenyl-3,4a,7,7,10a-pentamethyl-2-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxy-2,5,6,6a,8,9,10,10b-octahydro-1H-benzo[f]chromen-8-yl]oxy]-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol. Both of these compounds are ultimately targeted by Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP. The scores for the relationships are consistent, indicating a plausible biological connection. The evidence suggests that Sasanquasaponin I may act as a modulator of Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP through the regulation of Microtubule-associated protein 1B, which is a key intermediate in the pathway. The mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple distinct targets for Microtubule-associated protein 1B, suggesting a complex regulatory network. The strength of the evidence is moderate, and further experimental validation would be needed to confirm the direct interaction between Sasanquasaponin I and Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP."
